    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The draft looks good, but the last sentence about the travelers and the forest feels a bit weird for a corporate email. Can we just stick to the professional tone? Also, can you make the email a bit more specific? The candidate was particularly strong in distributed systems, so I'd like to mention that so it doesn't feel like a canned template.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's a revised draft:\n\n\"Dear [Candidate Name],\n\nI wanted to personally reach out to you regarding the status of your application for the Senior Developer position at our Austin office. We were impressed by your exceptional technical skills, particularly in distributed systems, and the time you took to showcase your expertise during the final round of interviews. Your ability to design and implement scalable, fault-tolerant systems aligns well with our company's needs, and we were excited to learn more about your approach to building resilient architectures.\n\nUnfortunately, after careful consideration, we have decided to move forward with another candidate whose skills and experience best align with our current needs. Please know that this decision was not made lightly, and we were truly impressed by your qualifications and passion for software development.\n\nWe appreciate the effort you put into preparing for our interviews and the time you invested in our process. We wish you the best of luck in your job search and are confident that your skills will be in high demand.\n\nBest regards,\n[Your Name]\"\n\nI've removed the forest analogy and added a specific mention of distributed systems to highlight the candidate's strengths. I've also kept the tone professional and kind, while still conveying the message that we're not moving forward with the candidate.",
